# Each composition's fusions, and whether every one of them has parts to add up. One row per
# composition: the fusions it declares, how many of them have at least one resolved part (`rank`),
# and how many have none (`left_null`).
#
# One count, read from each end
#   Each fusion with a resolved part adds up its own parts and no other fusion's, so the fusions
#   with parts are independent of one another, and `rank` is simply how many there are. The rest of
#   the declared fusions is `left_null`, so the two are one count read from each end, and
#   `declared = rank + left_null` says they add up.
#
# What a fusion with no parts would mean
#   A composed figure no assignment to the parts can produce: a declared fusion with nothing under
#   it at all, its figure owed by a sum over nothing. `asrt:Fusion/part` is `minOccurs="1"`, so the
#   filing cannot say it directly, and the state is reached the other way, through a part whose
#   filing this corpus does not hold.
#
# Which is why this counts resolved parts and the other law does not
#   `algebra/fusions_have_parts.sqlc` drives from the references a composer filed, and rightly:
#   whether a fusion named a part is a question about the document, and the answer does not depend
#   on what else was loaded. This relation asks the arithmetic's question instead, and a reference
#   to a filing nobody has adds nothing. So the two can part company, and the set where they do is
#   `composition/unresolved_parts.sqlc`. Neither is the other's check.
#
# Refused rather than corrected, as the layer graph's loops are
#   `checks/unresolved_part.sqlc` accuses a part that does not resolve, so a conforming corpus has
#   no such part, and `left_null` is zero in every conforming document. That is not the arithmetic
#   coming out flat; it is a rule holding it there, and the layer graph has no loops for the same
#   kind of reason. Both are laws that would have something to report if a rule were removed.
#
# Per composition, because that is who answers for it
#   A composition is one filer's claim, so a fusion missing its parts is that filer's document to
#   repair, even when the missing filing is somebody else's. One number for the corpus would name
#   nobody.
#
# Probe
#   `algebra/composition_image.sqlc` holds `left_null = 0`, with the declared count as its floor. In
#   a rolled-back transaction, delete the layer a part names, and this relation must show one on
#   that composition and nowhere else.
-- composition/fusions.sqlc against the fusions that have a resolved part.
SELECT f.filing                                         AS composition,
       count(*)                                         AS declared,
       count(k.composition)                             AS rank,
       count(*) - count(k.composition)                  AS left_null
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(rank/composition_kernel.sqlc)
) k ON k.composition = f.filing AND k.composed_layer = f.layer
GROUP BY f.filing
